{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Every one of 40 internal apps breaks in the same release</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A shared design system's v3 ships with a breaking change to a couple of component APIs, say, a <code>&lt;Button size="small"&gt;</code> prop is renamed to <code>size="sm"</code>. The platform team merges the change, publishes v3 to the internal registry, and moves on. The next morning, every one of the roughly 40 internal apps that depend on that package picks it up on a routine <code>npm install</code>, and every app that still used the old prop name breaks at once, not gradually, all together, because they're all consuming the exact same version bump.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      "Just publish v3" isn't really a versioning mistake, semver correctly signals a breaking change with a major bump, it's a rollout mistake: any app pinning a caret range like <code>^2.4.0</code> pulls the new major in on its next install, with no warning, no deprecation period, and no way to opt in on its own timeline. The bug is treating a shared design system's release like one app's own deploy, where a breaking change is scoped to a single team, when a design system with 40 consumers has a blast radius of 40 teams.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The deeper issue is that a single v3 tag conflates two things that should be decoupled: "the new API exists and is documented" and "every consumer is forced onto it right now." A design system with real leverage across an org needs a way to ship v3 as <em>available</em> without making it <em>mandatory</em> the moment someone runs <code>npm install</code>, otherwise every major version becomes a synchronized, org-wide fire drill instead of a rollout each team can pace for itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Deprecate first, ship a compatibility shim, let each app migrate on its own clock</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of a single hard cutover, a safer v3 keeps the old API working for a while. When an app uses the deprecated <code>size="small"</code> prop, the component doesn't throw, it silently maps <code>small</code> to <code>sm</code> internally and logs a one-time warning telling the team what changed and pointing at a codemod that fixes it automatically. The app keeps working today. The team gets a real window to run the codemod and update the prop name on its own schedule, not the design system team's.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is a compatibility shim: v3 ships the new implementation, but a thin translation layer inside the component maps old prop names and values to new ones, so the public API stays backward-compatible for a deprecation window even though the internals changed. Real systems automate the far side of that window too, Shopify's Polaris ships a <code>polaris-migrator</code> CLI that runs codemods against a consuming app's source to do the bulk of the rename/remove work automatically, and IBM's Carbon design system ships an equivalent <code>@carbon/upgrade</code> package for the same purpose. The shim buys time; the codemod removes the manual-labor cost of using it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The rollout shape that actually works is staged: publish v3 with the shim active by default so a plain caret-range install doesn't break anyone, instrument the shim to log real usage rather than just warn (so the platform team can watch deprecated-prop usage trend toward zero across the org instead of guessing from a changelog's reach), and only remove the shim, as its own release with its own changelog entry, once telemetry shows adoption is actually done. The version number stops being the forcing function; the shim's measured removal criterion is.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The second attempt shipped a dashboard instead of a Slack message</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A platform team owned a shared component library used by about 40 internal apps across three product lines. For v3, they renamed a handful of props on the Button and Modal components to match new design tokens. The first attempt: publish <code>v3.0.0</code>, post a Slack message. Within a day, 11 apps had failing CI and two were broken in production, teams that had run <code>npm install</code> for an unrelated dependency bump had no idea a design system update was even involved until their build failed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      For the redo, v3 shipped with every renamed prop still accepted through a shim, logging both a console warning and a hit to an internal telemetry endpoint on every deprecated-prop use. They also published a codemod, <code>npx @internal/ds-upgrade</code>, that rewrote the deprecated props automatically in a consuming app's source. Instead of one synchronized cutover, they got a live number: deprecated-prop usage across all 40 apps, trending down week over week as teams ran the codemod at their own pace.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      What made this durable rather than a one-time save was that the shim wasn't removed on a fixed calendar date, it was removed once telemetry showed zero real-world hits for two consecutive weeks, shipped as its own release with its own changelog entry ("the v2-compatible prop names are no longer accepted; run the v3 codemod if you haven't"). Two apps still hadn't migrated by then, the team reached out to those two directly instead of breaking all 40 again to force the issue.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

