{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The shim needs a removal criterion, or it becomes permanent debt</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A compatibility shim that never gets removed just becomes a second, permanently-supported API, every future engineer working on the component has to understand both the old and new prop names forever, and the docs have to describe two ways to do the same thing indefinitely.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix isn't a fixed deprecation date so much as an instrumented one, log every real hit of the shim, not just a console warning a developer might not even see, so the platform team has an actual answer to "is anyone still using this" instead of assuming a changelog entry was sufficient notice.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      There's a subtler footgun too: a dual-package hazard, where some consuming apps end up with two different major versions of the design system in their dependency tree simultaneously, one direct, one transitive via another internal package that hasn't updated yet. Bundlers that don't dedupe correctly can ship both, which for a component library often means duplicated CSS-in-JS runtime instances or duplicate context providers misbehaving in ways that are much harder to debug than a plain prop-name breakage. Worth checking for duplicate major versions in the dependency tree before calling a migration done, not just checking that the shim's usage count hit zero.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

